Day 267/300: 3Sum

Problem Statement #15

Problem Statement Explanation:

Given an integer array nums, return all the triplets [nums[i], nums[j], nums[k]] such that i != j, i != k, and j != k, and nums[i] + nums[j] + nums[k] == 0.
The solution set must not contain duplicate triplets.

Example:

Input: nums = [-1,0,1,2,-1,-4]
Output: [[-1,-1,2],[-1,0,1]]

Solution Approach:

The brute-force approach requires three nested loops (O(n^3)), which is too slow. 
By sorting the array first, we can use a Two-Pointer strategy to find pairs that complement a fixed value.

Step-by-Step Algorithm
Sort the Array: Sorting is essential to handle duplicates and to use the two-pointer logic.
Iterate through the array: Use a loop with index i to fix the first element of the triplet.
Skip Duplicates: If nums[i] is the same as nums[i-1], skip it to ensure unique triplets.
Two-Pointer Logic: * Set left = i + 1 and right = nums.length - 1.
Calculate sum = nums[i] + nums[left] + nums[right].

If sum == 0, add the triplet to the result and move both pointers, skipping any duplicate values for left and right.
If sum < 0, move the left pointer forward to increase the sum.
If sum > 0, move the right pointer backward to decrease the sum.

Here's the code:

import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> threeSum(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        Arrays.sort(nums);

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 2; i++) {
            if (i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) continue;
            if (nums[i] > 0) break;

            int left = i + 1;
            int right = nums.length - 1;

            while (left < right) {
                int sum = nums[i] + nums[left] + nums[right];

                if (sum == 0) {
                    res.add(Arrays.asList(nums[i], nums[left], nums[right]));
                    while (left < right && nums[left] == nums[left + 1]) left++;
                    while (left < right && nums[right] == nums[right - 1]) right--;
                    left++;
                    right--;
                } else if (sum < 0) {
                    left++;
                } else {
                    right--;
                }
            }
        }
        return res;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(n^2)
Space Complexity: O(logn) to O(n)



